+# Best AI Tools for Lawyers in 2026
+
+[![Awesome](https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/sindresorhus/awesome@main/media/badge.svg)](https://github.com/sindresorhus/awesome)
+[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT)
+![Topics](https://img.shields.io/github/stars/GagnDeep/awesome-best-ai-tools-for-lawyers-2026?style=social)
+
+> Best AI tools for lawyers in 2026 should be auditable, self-hostable, and linked to real public code. This list focuses only on open-source GitHub repositories that can help with legal document review, case-law analysis, contract workflows, OCR, private knowledge retrieval, and research automation. Every entry below has a `github.com` repository URL and excludes closed SaaS-only products.
+
+## Table of Contents
+- [TL;DR](#tldr)
+- [Why This List](#why-this-list)
+- [Open Source Tools](#open-source-tools)
+- [FAQ](#faq)
+- [Contributing](#contributing)
+- [License](#license)
+
+## TL;DR
+- **For legal document review and extraction:** [OpenContracts](https://github.com/JSv4/OpenContracts), [Docling](https://github.com/DS4SD/docling), and [Unstructured](https://github.com/Unstructured-IO/unstructured) are strong open-source building blocks.
+- **For private legal copilots:** [Open WebUI](https://github.com/open-webui/open-webui), [AnythingLLM](https://github.com/Mintplex-Labs/anything-llm), [LibreChat](https://github.com/danny-avila/LibreChat), and [DocsGPT](https://github.com/arc53/DocsGPT) are the most practical self-hosted front ends.
+- **For legal NLP and judgment analysis:** [Opennyai](https://github.com/OpenNyAI/Opennyai), [legal_NER](https://github.com/Legal-NLP-EkStep/legal_NER), and [rhetorical-role-baseline](https://github.com/Legal-NLP-EkStep/rhetorical-role-baseline) are among the few clearly lawyer-relevant public repos.
+- **For scanned filings and exhibits:** [OCRmyPDF](https://github.com/ocrmypdf/OCRmyPDF), [PaddleOCR](https://github.com/PaddlePaddle/PaddleOCR), and [Tesseract](https://github.com/tesseract-ocr/tesseract) remain essential.
+
+## Why This List
+Law firms, solo practitioners, in-house teams, and legal ops groups often need AI systems they can inspect, self-host, and adapt to sensitive workflows. A useful list of the best AI tools for lawyers in 2026 cannot just repeat generic SaaS recommendations; it needs repositories with visible licenses, active GitHub histories, and code that can be audited for privacy, reproducibility, and long-term control.
+
+This repository therefore prioritizes public GitHub projects that help lawyers work with contracts, pleadings, judgments, regulations, discovery files, and internal knowledge bases. Where the legal-specific open-source market is still thin, the list includes adjacent document AI and retrieval tools that are widely usable in legal practice and notes that gap honestly.
+
+## Open Source Tools
+
+### Legal AI, Contract Analysis, and Case-Law NLP
+
+#### [OpenContracts](https://github.com/JSv4/OpenContracts)
+> **Description:** OpenContracts describes itself as a free and open-source document analytics platform built for unstructured documents. Its README emphasizes document management, annotation, vector embeddings, pluggable analyzers, layout parsing, custom metadata schemas, and agent-style bulk extraction over PDFs and text files. For legal teams this is one of the clearest GitHub-native options for clause review, redaction workflows, corpus search, and structured extraction over large contract sets. GitHub shows a GPL-3.0 codebase with a TypeScript- and Python-heavy stack. Last visible GitHub release/activity: 2025-10-26. Stars: 961. Primary language: TypeScript. License: GPL-3.0.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/JSv4/OpenContracts](https://github.com/JSv4/OpenContracts)
+- **Stars:** 961 ⭐
+- **Language:** TypeScript
+- **License:** GPL-3.0
+- **Last Commit:** 2025-10-26
+- **Category:** legal-tech, contract-analysis
+- **Best for:** open-source contract review and document extraction
+
+---
+
+#### [LawGlance](https://github.com/lawglance/lawglance)
+> **Description:** LawGlance is a free, open-source, RAG-based AI legal assistant focused on making legal guidance accessible. Its README highlights retrieval-augmented generation over legal texts, a people-centric design, Streamlit delivery, Redis-backed caching, and coverage of Indian legal materials such as the Constitution and recent criminal statutes. This is useful as a reference architecture for lawyers who want a narrow-domain assistant rather than a generic chatbot. GitHub shows an Apache-2.0 repository with Python as the primary language. The latest visible commit page shows activity on 2025-07-06. Stars: 239. Primary language: Python. License: Apache-2.0.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/lawglance/lawglance](https://github.com/lawglance/lawglance)
