+## TL;DR
+- **For legal document review and extraction:** [OpenContracts](https://github.com/JSv4/OpenContracts), [Docling](https://github.com/DS4SD/docling), and [Unstructured](https://github.com/Unstructured-IO/unstructured) are strong open-source building blocks.
+- **For private legal copilots:** [Open WebUI](https://github.com/open-webui/open-webui), [AnythingLLM](https://github.com/Mintplex-Labs/anything-llm), [LibreChat](https://github.com/danny-avila/LibreChat), and [DocsGPT](https://github.com/arc53/DocsGPT) are the most practical self-hosted front ends.
+- **For legal NLP and judgment analysis:** [Opennyai](https://github.com/OpenNyAI/Opennyai), [legal_NER](https://github.com/Legal-NLP-EkStep/legal_NER), and [rhetorical-role-baseline](https://github.com/Legal-NLP-EkStep/rhetorical-role-baseline) are among the few clearly lawyer-relevant public repos.
+- **For scanned filings and exhibits:** [OCRmyPDF](https://github.com/ocrmypdf/OCRmyPDF), [PaddleOCR](https://github.com/PaddlePaddle/PaddleOCR), and [Tesseract](https://github.com/tesseract-ocr/tesseract) remain essential.
+
+## Why This List
+Law firms, solo practitioners, in-house teams, and legal ops groups often need AI systems they can inspect, self-host, and adapt to sensitive workflows. A useful list of the best AI tools for lawyers in 2026 cannot just repeat generic SaaS recommendations; it needs repositories with visible licenses, active GitHub histories, and code that can be audited for privacy, reproducibility, and long-term control.
+
+This repository therefore prioritizes public GitHub projects that help lawyers work with contracts, pleadings, judgments, regulations, discovery files, and internal knowledge bases. Where the legal-specific open-source market is still thin, the list includes adjacent document AI and retrieval tools that are widely usable in legal practice and notes that gap honestly.
